    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Packaging is the least-specified line item in most wire mesh purchase orders, and the source of the most avoidable disputes. Rolls arrive with abraded coatings from bands biting into the wire; pallets arrive soaked through a container that "looked dry" at loading in Tianjin; panels arrive bent because one thin steel strap was asked to hold 800 kg against a forklift impact. Every one of these failures was decided on paper weeks before the ship left port.
      </p>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        This guide gives you the packing specifications to write into your PO, the loading patterns that protect cargo in transit, and the claim procedure when something still goes wrong. For the surrounding import steps, see our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-import-wire-mesh-from-china/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">importing wire mesh from China</a>; for fitting the load, use the <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">container loading calculator</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Roll vs Panel -->
    <section id="roll-vs-panel" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Roll vs Panel Packing: What to Specify</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Rolled products (woven mesh, welded mesh rolls, chain link, hex netting, <a href="/products/chain-link-fence/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">chain link fence fabric</a>) and flat products (welded mesh panels, fence panels, <a href="/products/welded-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">welded wire mesh sheets</a>) fail in completely different ways, so they need different packing specs.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Item</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Roll Packing Spec</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Panel Packing Spec</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Inner wrap</td>
              <td class="p-3">Waterproof kraft paper, min 80 g/m², fully taped seams</td>
              <td class="p-3">VCI paper or PE foam between panel layers</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Outer wrap</td>
              <td class="p-3">Woven PP bag, stitched and taped; UV-stabilized for deck stowage</td>
              <td class="p-3">Stretch film 3 layers + outer PE sheet or shrink hood</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Strapping</td>
              <td class="p-3">4–6 PET or steel straps across roll diameter, edge protectors under bands</td>
              <td class="p-3">Through-panel steel strapping (not surface-only), minimum 4 bands per stack</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Pallet</td>
              <td class="p-3">Not required for rolls under ~80 kg; heavier rolls palletized flat, max 2 layers</td>
              <td class="p-3">Mandatory. Fumigated ISPM-15 hardwood or pressed-wood pallets, entry on 4 sides</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Edge protection</td>
              <td class="p-3">Cardboard or plastic angle guards at every strap contact point</td>
              <td class="p-3">Corner boards on all 4 vertical corners of each stack</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Labeling</td>
              <td class="p-3">1 label per roll: PO number, spec, roll length, gross/net weight, batch</td>
              <td class="p-3">1 label per pallet face + packing list in waterproof document pouch</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The two specs most often omitted—and most often regretted—are <strong>edge protectors under straps</strong> and <strong>through-panel strapping</strong>. A bare steel band tightened to 200 kg of tension crushes wire and cracks zinc coating at every contact point; you will find rust blooms exactly there six months after installation. Through-panel strapping means the band passes through gaps in the mesh or around the pallet frame, so panels cannot slide even if the film is torn.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Moisture -->
    <section id="moisture" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Moisture and Corrosion Protection for Sea Freight</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        A shipping container is a steel box moving through ocean air with temperature swings of 20–30 °C between day and night. Warm humid air condenses on the cooler container walls and roof—this is <strong>container rain</strong>, and it pools on the floor under the cargo or drips onto the top layer. Galvanized mesh tolerates this reasonably well for short sailings, but any exposed cut edge, weld point, or abraded coating becomes a rust site within one long voyage. Stainless mesh is not immune either—tea staining and surface rust from chloride-bearing condensation are common complaints on 304 mesh arriving in humid ports.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Spec the moisture package by transit length and product sensitivity:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Under ~15 days transit (Asia–West Coast, China–Europe rail):</strong> Waterproof kraft inner + woven bag outer is adequate for galvanized rolls. Add one 1 kg calcium chloride desiccant strip per 3 CBM for panels.</li>
        <li><strong>15–35 days transit (Asia–East Coast, Asia–Northern Europe via Suez):</strong> Add VCI (vapor corrosion inhibitor) paper interleaved with panels or wrapped around roll ends, plus desiccant strips at 1 kg per 2 CBM, hung from the container lashing points—not laid on the cargo.</li>
        <li><strong>Long-haul or deck-stowed cargo, tropical destination, or light electro-galvanized product:</strong> Full moisture barrier: PE liner around the cargo block, container floor covered with kraft paper or dunnage to break capillary contact, 8–10 desiccant strips for a 20ft box, and request below-deck stowage on the booking.</li>
        <li><strong>Stainless steel mesh:</strong> Keep it dry and salt-free; specify VCI paper and plastic end caps, and prohibit floor loading—contact with wet container floors is the main cause of tea staining.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Cheap Insurance</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          A 20ft container's full desiccant + VCI package costs $40–90. The average rust claim on a mixed mesh shipment runs $2,000–8,000 once you count replacement material, air freight for the shortfall, and re-processing labor. Write the moisture spec into the PO as a line item with quantities, not as a verbal "please protect against rust."
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Strapping & pallets -->
    <section id="strapping" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Strapping, Pallets, and Labeling Norms</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Pallet norms exist because your cargo will be handled by at least four parties after it leaves the factory—trucking, port, terminal, and destination warehouse—and every one of them will move it with machinery that does not know what is inside. Pallet specs that survive this chain:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>ISPM-15 compliance is mandatory</strong> for all solid-wood pallets in international trade. The stamp (HT for heat-treated, or KD) must be visible on two opposite faces. Pressed-wood and plastic pallets are exempt but specify them explicitly.</li>
        <li><strong>Deck boards:</strong> minimum 15 mm thickness, no spacing over 70 mm for panels under 1 m width—narrow feet fall through wide deck gaps.</li>
        <li><strong>Height limit:</strong> keep pallet stacks under 1.8 m for stable double-stacking and to stay within warehouse racking limits.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight limit:</strong> design for 1,200–1,500 kg per pallet for dense mesh; a pallet that fails under a forklift blade takes the whole stack down.</li>
        <li><strong>Strapping:</strong> PET (polyester) strapping at 16–19 mm width is the export standard—stronger than PP, no rust staining like steel. Steel strapping is acceptable for dense coils where PET elongates; either way, edge protection is non-negotiable.</li>
        <li><strong>Labels:</strong> one per roll or per pallet face showing PO, item, dimensions, roll count, gross and net weight, and country of origin. The packing list goes in a waterproof document pouch on the outside of the load or pallet, never loose inside the container.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Ask the factory for <strong>photos of the packing line</strong> before the first shipment: rolls being wrapped, bands being tensioned, pallets being stacked. Ten minutes of photo review catches 80% of packing failures before they are sealed in a container.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Loading -->
    <section id="loading" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Container Loading Patterns for Wire Mesh</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        How cargo sits in the box matters as much as how it is packed. The governing constraints are weight distribution (ports and insurers require longitudinal balance), stability against the rolling and racking forces of a ship, and protection of the lightest cargo from the heaviest.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Weight first, volume second.</strong> Heavy rolls or panel pallets load first against the front bulkhead, with weight distributed side-to-side within 60/40. Never leave one side of a container 2 tons heavier—it stresses the box and triggers port weight-shifting inspections.</li>
        <li><strong>Rolls on end vs flat:</strong> hex netting and light chain link ship well standing on end (tight wall-to-wall packing, no crushing). Woven mesh rolls over ~60 kg and any roll with a fragile coating ship flat, stacked max 2 layers with timber dunnage between layers so bands never bear on the roll below.</li>
        <li><strong>No voids behind the doors.</strong> The back third of the container takes the worst handling shocks. If the load cannot reach the doors, build a timber bulkhead or brace—never leave a gap the door-end pallets can accelerate into.</li>
        <li><strong>Floor protection.</strong> Container floors get condensation and residual seawater. One layer of kraft paper or thin dunnage under the first tier breaks capillary contact and prevents floor rust rings on galvanized product.</li>
        <li><strong>Lash long loads.</strong> Any load over ~2 m of unsupported length (long panel pallets, bundled tubes) gets cross-lashed to the container lashing points. Rolling at sea multiplies load-shift forces far beyond forklift handling.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Build the loading plan before the goods reach the port, not in the truck yard. The <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">container loading calculator</a> lets you verify roll diameters, stack heights, and total payload against container limits, and the resulting plan—sent to the factory in advance—becomes the packing instruction their warehouse follows.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Parameter</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">20ft GP</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">40ft High Cube</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Internal volume</td>
              <td class="p-3">~33.2 CBM</td>
              <td class="p-3">~76.4 CBM</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Max payload (mesh cargo)</td>
              <td class="p-3">~21,700 kg (28,200 kg box limit minus tare ~2,230 kg; confirm road limits)</td>
              <td class="p-3">~26,500 kg (30,480 kg limit minus tare ~3,900 kg)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Typical mesh load</td>
              <td class="p-3">25–28 t of dense rolls/panels (weighs out) or ~30 CBM of light mesh (cubes out)</td>
              <td class="p-3">~26 t max weight, but volume-limited mesh loads reach only 55–75% full</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Door opening</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.34 m W × 2.28 m H</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.34 m W × 2.58 m H</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Practical mesh rule</td>
              <td class="p-3">Best value for dense mesh under ~26 t</td>
              <td class="p-3">Only worth it for light mesh you can stack to 2.4 m+; otherwise air freight in a box</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The 40ft trap: light mesh loaded to only chest height leaves 30+ CBM of paid-for air. Either stack taller with proper dunnage and strapping, or split the program across two 20ft boxes—smaller containers often load denser because every roll can be walked to the walls.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        And photograph the loading. Request from the factory: <strong>empty container</strong> (clean, dry, no holes in roof or door seals), <strong>25% loaded</strong>, <strong>75% loaded</strong>, <strong>full load with doors open</strong>, and <strong>sealed container showing seal number</strong>. These five photos are your evidence for both damage claims and insurance disputes, and they cost the loader two minutes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Claims -->
    <section id="claims" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Receiving Inspection and the Damage Claims Process</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Claims are won or lost in the first 48 hours after container opening. Insurance policies and carrier terms both impose short notice windows—marine cargo policies commonly require immediate notice and formal claim submission within days of delivery. Build this sequence into your warehouse procedure:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Receiving Checklist</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Photograph the sealed container: seal number, doors, exterior condition before opening</li>
            <li>☐ Photograph the interior before moving anything—load shift, water, torn wrap</li>
            <li>☐ Record seal number against the packing list and B/L</li>
            <li>☐ Check for water damage, floor rust rings, condensation evidence</li>
            <li>☐ Count rolls/pallets against the packing list on the spot</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Unpack damaged or suspect items immediately; photograph damage with a measuring tape or label in frame</li>
            <li>☐ Micrometer-check wire diameter and coating on suspect rolls</li>
            <li>☐ Note every discrepancy on the delivery receipt before signing—"received with exception" language</li>
            <li>☐ Notify the factory, forwarder, and insurer within 24 hours with photo evidence</li>
            <li>☐ Submit formal claim documents within the policy window (typically 7–30 days)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The documentation pack for a claim usually requires: the commercial invoice and packing list, the bill of lading, the insurance certificate (if you insured the cargo—do, at ~0.3% of value it is cheap), the delivery receipt with noted exceptions, the damage photographs, and a repair-or-replace cost statement. Claims filed with clean photo sequences and timely notice resolve in weeks; claims filed from memory months later resolve never.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Two final points. First, <strong>concealed damage</strong> (rust or coating damage hidden inside the wrap that only appears at unpacking) still gets claimed—notify within 48 hours of unpacking and preserve the packaging as evidence. Second, if the same damage pattern repeats across shipments (same band crush marks, same wet corner), the root cause is the packing spec, not the carrier. Fix it in the next PO with the specifications from this article.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Do I need fumigated pallets for wire mesh shipments?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            If the pallets are solid wood, yes—ISPM-15 heat treatment (HT stamp) is legally required at most destination ports, and non-compliant wood can be refused entry or destroyed at your expense. Pressed-wood (chipboard/MDF) and plastic pallets are exempt. Specify "ISPM-15 HT hardwood pallets" or "plastic pallets" in the PO so there is no ambiguity at the packing line.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is the standard waterproof paper and woven bag wrapping enough for sea freight?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For short transit (under ~15 days) with hot-dip galvanized product, usually yes. For longer sailings, tropical destinations, or any light electro-galvanized or stainless mesh, it is not—add VCI paper and container desiccant strips, and keep cargo off the container floor. The upgrade costs a few dollars per ton and prevents rust claims worth far more.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How long do I have to file a damage claim on a wire mesh shipment?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Notice windows are short: notify the carrier and your forwarder immediately (same day) and the insurer within 24–48 hours. Formal claim documents are typically due within 7–30 days depending on the policy and carrier. Keep the damaged goods and packaging until the claim is resolved—insurers routinely reject claims where evidence was discarded.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Should wire mesh rolls be shipped standing up or lying down?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Light rolls (hex netting, chain link, mesh under ~60 kg) pack best on end against the container walls—maximum density, no crush risk. Heavy rolls and any roll with delicate coating ship flat, maximum two layers, with timber dunnage between layers. Never mix the two orientations loosely in the same void; shifting loads destroy packaging fast.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What photos should I require from the factory before shipping?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Five minimum: the empty container (clean and dry), 25% loaded, 75% loaded, the full load with doors open showing the packing arrangement and bracing, and the closed container with the seal number visible. Add close-ups of strapping with edge protectors and labels. These photos become your evidence chain for both quality and damage disputes at destination.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

